    "title": "What time are the Oscars? How to watch, stream red carpet, 2026 show",
    "text": "March 15, 2026, 11:22 a.m. ETThe 98th Academy Awards will take place on Sunday, March 15, 2026.The awards ceremony begins at 7 p.m. ET and will air on ABC. Red carpet coverage begins at 3:30 p.m.Viewers can stream the show on services like Hulu, Fubo, and YouTube TV.The Academy Awards are one of film’s biggest nights, and the lights, cameras, and action for the 98th edition all happen on Sunday.The Oscars traditionally close out the film awards season, celebrating achievements and major contenders across best picture, acting, directing, technical achievements and more. The show airs on Sunday, March 15, giving fans a chance to see red carpet arrivals and who goes home with a coveted gold statuette.Here's how to watch the ceremony.When are the 2026 Oscars?The Academy Awards will take place Sunday, March 15, 2026.What time does the 2026 Oscars ceremony start?The awards ceremony begins at 7 p.m. ET, with major red-carpet coverage starting at 3:30 p.m. ET on ABC.How to watch the 2026 Academy Awards live: See channels, streamingHere's how to watch the 2026 Oscars:Date: March 15, 2026Time: 7 p.m. ETChannel: ABC and the ABC appStreaming: Hulu, Fubo, YouTube TV, AT&T TVLocation: Dolby Theatre at Ovation Hollywood, Los Angeles, CaliforniaWatch the Oscars on FuboOnly three winners ever rejected their Oscars, including one from OhioOnly three people in Oscars history have declined their statuettes — actors George C. Scott and Marlon Brando, and screenwriter‑director Dudley Nichols, who was born in Wapakoneta, Ohio, in 1895.Nichols won the Oscar for \"The Informer,\" a 1935 film, and he initially refused to accept the award because of labor and union disputes with the Academy, according to USA TODAY. He eventually accepted the award in 1938. Nichols, a co‑founder of the Screenwriters Guild, served as the organization's president from 1937 to 1938.How to stream best picture nominees before the OscarsMovie fans who might have missed seeing some of the 10 best picture nominees can catch up with a weekend of binge-watching across streaming platforms.Providers such as Netflix, Amazon, Apple TV, Hulu, Peacock and Fandango offer opportunities to watch the movies at home, according to USA TODAY.",
